[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged as one issue: the parser reads dates month first, and our
tenants write the day first. Read report by report, the merged reports show two problems, and the
queued fix closes only one of them.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is not one issue. Each problem below has its own ticket now. The date-order fix leaves the
one-day moves open.
</Callout>

## What the reports show

<ProblemTable
  ticket={4471}
  tickets={{
    swap: 'https://github.com/luca-gugs/what-it-shows/issues/1',
    shift: 'https://github.com/luca-gugs/what-it-shows/issues/3',
  }}
/>

## How to tell them apart

- **`swap`**: the report shows day and month swapped ("Our 7 April reminder went out on 4 July"). A
  day-first date was read month first. `causes.ts` splits these into `parser` and `hint` by channel
  and booking form, but the reports do not: they show the same swap, whichever way they came in. One
  problem, one ticket. The booking-v3 form's `MM/DD/YYYY` hint is part of its fix.
- **`shift`**: no swap at all. The date moves by one day: "Tuesday 6 October showed as Monday",
  "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday." The desk read these as a one-day move
  (`desk-notes.json`) but filed them under `parser` by tenant. Swapping day and month never moves a
  date by one day, so the date-order fix cannot close them. It looks like a time-zone conversion of
  the booking date.

Neither the subject line nor the intake tag tells them apart. Read the body.

## How the count was made

1. Take the export of #4471 and keep one report per id. The ticket system exports pages that
   overlap, so some reports come twice.
2. Take out the reports the desk read as a one-day move: they are `shift`.
3. Every other report comes from a day-first tenant and shows, or describes, the swap: `swap`.

The numbers on this page are computed from the export when the page renders
(`#lib/kb/problems.ts`), so they follow the data as the desk keeps merging.

## What this changes

- #4471 is not "known, one fix queued". The queued fix covers `swap`. `shift` needs its own fix and
  its own owner.
- Merging on the subject alone ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) cannot keep
  these apart. That decision stays as it is; a new record should replace it.

